<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Túpac Inca Yupanqui</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1471">1471</a>–<a href="/years/1493">1493</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Topa Inca Yupanqui</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Túpac <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Inca</a> Yupanqui was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pachacuti">Pachacuti</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapa-inca">Sapa Inca</a> from about 1471 to 1493. First as his father's general and then as ruler, he enormously enlarged the Inca Empire, conquering the Chimú kingdom on the north coast, the Cañari and peoples of Ecuador, the Chincha and much of Chile. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huayna-capac">Huayna Capac</a> succeeded him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tupac-inca-yupanqui.jpg" alt="Túpac Inca Yupanqui" width="320" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Felipe Huaman Puma de Ayala · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tupac-inca-yupanqui-small.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Túpac Inca Yupanqui</b>, first as general and from about 1471 as ruler, conquered the Kingdom of Chimor on the north coast and pushed the frontiers into Ecuador and south into Chile, while Huayna Capac, from about 1493, extended rule to the north.</blockquote><a href="/empire/inca-empire">Inca Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Chimor’s great rival proved to be the Incas, whose armies, led by <b>Topa Inca Yupanqui</b>, son of Pachacuti, overcame the kingdom around 1470.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-chimor">Kingdom of Chimor · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>There is a consensus among archaeologists that Pachacutec ordered the royal estate after his conquest of the middle and lower Urubamba, as part of a wider programme of royal estates along the river, and construction appears to date from his reign and that of <b>Túpac Inca Yupanqui</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/machu-picchu">Machu Picchu · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>A lineage that backed the losing side in a succession struggle could be destroyed: in 1532 Atahualpa’s generals took Cusco, burned the mummy of <b>Túpac Inca Yupanqui</b> and killed many of his lineage, who had supported Huáscar.</blockquote><a href="/societies/inca-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Inca Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1463 the Inca ruler <b>Túpac Inca Yupanqui</b> conquered the Cañari after hard resistance, and the Incas built Tomebamba on the site of Guapondelig.</blockquote><a href="/state/canari-confederation">Cañari Confederation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Inca rulers <b>Túpac Inca Yupanqui</b> and Huayna Capac fought long campaigns in the north.</blockquote><a href="/state/caranqui">Caranqui</a></li></ul></section>
